Title:
GVCIDrv64.sys LPE via Arbitrary Physical Memory R/W (Gigabyte BIOS Utility Driver)

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
This repository contains a local privilege escalation (LPE) exploit and helper tooling targeting Gigabyte’s signed kernel driver `GVCIDrv64.sys` shipped with a BIOS utility. The driver exposes a device `\\.\GVCIDrv64` without a security descriptor, allowing any unprivileged user to open it and invoke IOCTLs that map physical memory and perform raw I/O port reads/writes with no access control. By rewriting a PCI device’s BAR0 via the port IOCTL and then invoking the “map 32MB” IOCTL, the exploit gains arbitrary physical memory read/write from user mode without kernel-mode code execution. It then scans physical memory for the SYSTEM process (PID 4), steals its token, overwrites the current process token, and spawns a SYSTEM shell (data-only token swap). The write-up notes practical constraints (BAR0 is 32-bit → <4GB physical addressing; risk of BSOD if a NIC BAR is swapped while interrupts occur) and provides a standalone physical memory dump/read-write tool (`arb_rw.c`). The issue was reported to Gigabyte; they removed the vulnerable driver and replaced it with `GvVbiosDrv64.sys` (Advisory 2454).

Technical Focus:
- Abusing signed third-party kernel drivers (“BYOVD”-style) for LPE
- IOCTL access control failures / missing device security descriptor
- PCI BAR (Base Address Register) manipulation and MMIO mapping
- Arbitrary physical memory read/write from user mode
- Windows token stealing via physical memory scanning (SYSTEM token swap)
- I/O port access (in/out instructions via driver interface)

Use Cases:
- Local privilege escalation on systems with Gigabyte BIOS utility installed (vulnerable driver present/loaded)
- Post-exploitation privilege escalation path leveraging a signed driver
- Physical memory dumping/inspection from a low-privileged context (lab/forensics/research)
- Driver security assessment example: chaining “benign” IOCTL features into full read/write primitive

Keywords:
GVCIDrv64.sys, Gigabyte, BIOS utility, signed driver, LPE, IOCTL, \\.\GVCIDrv64, missing security descriptor, arbitrary physical memory read/write, PCI, BAR0, MMIO mapping, I/O ports, token stealing, SYSTEM token, PID 4, SCM driver load, Windows 10 x64 22H2, VMware, Security Advisory 2454